%I A129289 #23 Nov 25 2026 21:27:04
%S A129289 0,44,95,219,455,744,1460,2832,4515,8687,16683,26492,50808,97412,
%T A129289 154583,296307,567935,901152,1727180,3310344,5252475,10066919,
%U A129289 19294275,30613844,58674480,112455452,178430735,341980107,655438583,1039970712,1993206308,3820176192
%N A129289 Nonnegative values x of solutions (x, y) to the Diophantine equation x^2+(x+73)^2 = y^2.
%C A129289 Also values x of Pythagorean triples (x, x+73, y).
%C A129289 Corresponding values y of solutions (x, y) are in A160041.
%C A129289 lim_{n -> infinity} a(n)/a(n-3) = 3+2*sqrt(2).
%C A129289 lim_{n -> infinity} a(n)/a(n-1) = (89+36*sqrt(2))/73 for n mod 3 = {1, 2}.
%C A129289 lim_{n -> infinity} a(n)/a(n-1) = (5907+1802*sqrt(2))/73^2 for n mod 3 = 0.
%C A129289 The nonnegative solutions of x^2 + (x+73)^2 = y^2 form exactly three Pell orbits with canonical seeds (x,y) = (0,73), (44,125), and (95,193). In the notation of the cited Pell-orbit catalog, H(73) = 3 and P(73) = 2: three solution streams are needed to obtain the increasing x-sequence, and two of these streams consist of primitive Pythagorean triples. - _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 17 2026
%H A129289 G. C. Greubel, <a href="/A129289/b129289.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A129289 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23592032">A Unified Pell-Orbit Theory for the Diophantine Equation x^2+(x+d)^2=y^2: Complete Parametrization, Recurrences, and an OEIS Catalogue</a>, Zenodo (2026), Section 11.4.10.
%H A129289 <a href="/index/Rec#order_07">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (1,0,6,-6,0,-1,1).
%F A129289 a(n) = 6*a(n-3) -a(n-6) +146 for n > 6; a(1)=0, a(2)=44, a(3)=95, a(4)=219, a(5)=455, a(6)=744.
%F A129289 G.f.: x*(44+51*x+124*x^2-28*x^3-17*x^4-28*x^5)/((1-x)*(1-6*x^3+x^6)).
%F A129289 a(3*k+1) = 73*A001652(k) for k >= 0.
%e A129289 The three canonical seeds give the triples (0,73,73), (44,117,125), and (95,168,193). Applying the Pell transformation (x,y) -> (3*x + 2*y + 73, 4*x + 3*y + 146) gives the next triples (219,292,365), (455,528,697), and (744,817,1105), respectively. - _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 17 2026
%t A129289 Select[Range[0,100000],IntegerQ[Sqrt[#^2+(#+73)^2]]&] (* _Vladimir Joseph Stephan Orlovsky_, Feb 02 2012 *)
%t A129289 (* Alternative: *)
%t A129289 LinearRecurrence[{1,0,6,-6,0,-1,1},{0,44,95,219,455,744,1460},70] (* _Vladimir Joseph Stephan Orlovsky_, Feb 02 2012 *)
%o A129289 (PARI) {forstep(n=0, 100000000, [3, 1], if(issquare(2*n^2+146*n+5329), print1(n, ", ")))}
%o A129289 (Magma) m:=25; R<x>:=PowerSeriesRing(Integers(), m); [0] cat Coefficients(R!(x*(44+51*x+124*x^2-28*x^3-17*x^4-28*x^5)/((1-x)*(1-6*x^3+x^6)))); // _G. C. Greubel_, May 07 2018
%o A129289 (Python)
%o A129289 def a129289(n):
%o A129289     streams = [(0, 73), (44, 125), (95, 193)]
%o A129289     for _ in range(n - 1):
%o A129289         i = min(range(3), key=lambda j: streams[j][0])
%o A129289         x, y = streams[i]
%o A129289         streams[i] = (3*x + 2*y + 73, 4*x + 3*y + 146)
%o A129289     return min(x for x, y in streams)
%o A129289 print([a129289(n) for n in range(1, 41)]) # _Farhad Banazadeh_, Nov 17 2026
%Y A129289 Cf. A160041, A129288, A001652, A156035 (decimal expansion of 3+2*sqrt(2)), A160042 (decimal expansion of (89+36*sqrt(2))/73), A160043 (decimal expansion of (5907+1802*sqrt(2))/73^2).
%Y A129289 Cf. A076296 (d=7), A118120 (d=17), A118674 (d=31), A129288 (d=41), A118673 (d=71), and A118675 (d=47) for related fixed-difference equations and Pell-orbit decompositions.
